{"term":"прелюбодей","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"prelyubodey","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"adultery","concept":"fornicator","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"An adulterer; a man who commits sexual infidelity against his marriage vows, derived from Church Slavonic and central to Orthodox moral theology.","english_term":"adulterer","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"More specific than 'fornicator': прелюбодей denotes transgression within marriage (пре- = across a boundary), not merely sex outside marriage. Core vocabulary of Russian Orthodox moral discourse and Bible translation."}
{"term":"блудница","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"bludnitsa","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"harlot","concept":"sex_worker_archaic","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":30,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":75,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A harlot or sexually immoral woman; the feminine form of блудник, used extensively in biblical texts and Church Slavonic literature.","english_term":"harlot","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Most recognizable as the блудница who anoints Christ's feet in the Gospels. From блуд (fornication/straying). Carries heavy biblical weight; not merely a prostitute but a woman whose entire identity is defined by sexual sin and moral ruin."}
{"term":"распутство","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"rasputstvo","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"debauchery","concept":"debauchery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":35,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Licentiousness or dissolute behavior; habitual sexual promiscuity and moral looseness combined with indulgence in earthly pleasures.","english_term":"debauchery","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"The surname Rasputin (Распутин) is derived from this root, making the word culturally loaded. Slightly more focused on promiscuity and loose living than разврат, which connotes active moral corruption. Used in literary and journalistic contexts."}
{"term":"похоть","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"pokhot'","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"lust","concept":"lust","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":30,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":85,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Lust; intense carnal desire; one of the seven deadly sins in Orthodox theology, denoting uncontrolled sexual appetite.","english_term":"lust","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Core term in Russian Orthodox moral theology; appears throughout Church Slavonic Bible translations and remains widely recognizable. Slightly more specific to sexual desire than страсть (passion), which is broader and not exclusively sexual."}
{"term":"любодеяние","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"lyubodeyaniye","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"fornication","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":45,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Fornication; illicit sexual intercourse between unmarried persons; a Church Slavonic compound carrying strong moral and religious condemnation.","english_term":"fornication","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Compound of люб- (love/pleasure) + деяние (act/deed). More specifically ecclesiastical than блудодеяние; appears primarily in pre-20th century religious texts and biblical translations. Effectively extinct in modern spoken Russian."}
{"term":"содомский грех","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"sodomsky grekh","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sodomy","concept":"sodomy","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":40,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The sin of Sodom; a phrase explicitly framing sodomy as a biblical transgression deserving divine punishment, drawing on the destruction of Sodom in Genesis.","english_term":"the sin of Sodom","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Carries heavier moral condemnation than the standalone legal term мужеложство by invoking divine retribution. Used in Orthodox homiletics and conservative political rhetoric. Loads the act with eschatological weight beyond mere legal prohibition."}
{"term":"мужеложство","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"muzhelozhstvo","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sodomy","concept":"sodomy","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":50,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Male homosexual intercourse; the legal and religious term used in Soviet criminal law (Article 121) that criminalized sex between men until 1993.","english_term":"buggery","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally 'man-lying' (муж=man, ложство from ложе=bed). Unlike the broader English 'sodomy,' мужеложство is restricted specifically to male-male sex. Decriminalized in 1993 but remains the formal legal-historical term and appears in contemporary conservative religious rhetoric."}
{"term":"целомудрие","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"tselomudrie","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"chastity","concept":"sexual_abstinence","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":0,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Chastity; sexual purity; the virtue of refraining from sinful sexual conduct, a core concept in Orthodox Christian ethics.","english_term":"chastity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally 'whole-wisdom' (цело=whole/intact + мудрость=wisdom); implies both sexual purity and mental integrity — not merely abstinence but the positive virtue of sexual self-mastery. Widely used in contemporary Russian Orthodox discourse and conservative political rhetoric."}
{"term":"девство","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"devstvo","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"virginity","concept":"defloration","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":5,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":58,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Maidenhood; the archaic literary term for the state of female virginity, emphasizing the social and moral condition of the unmarried virgin girl.","english_term":"maidenhood","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Concept mapped (defloration) describes the act of losing virginity; this word describes the prior state — no registry concept covers virginity-as-state. More poetic and archaic than девственность; emphasizes the maiden's social role and honor. Frequent in folk tales, byliny, and 18th–19th century literature."}
{"term":"девственность","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"devstvennost'","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"virginity","concept":"defloration","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":5,"technical":40,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Virginity; the standard literary and semi-clinical Russian term for the state of never having had sexual intercourse.","english_term":"virginity","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Concept mapped (defloration) describes the act of losing virginity; this word names the prior state. The standard modern term — more clinical and less archaic than девство. Commonly used in education, literature, and public health; unlike other words in this section it remains in active use."}
{"term":"непотребство","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"nepotrebstvo","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"indecency","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":30,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Obscenity or indecency; sexually offensive or morally objectionable behavior; an archaic legal and religious term for sexual misconduct.","english_term":"indecency","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Literally 'un-need-ness' (не + потреба = need/use); behavior that should not exist or be tolerated. Used in pre-revolutionary Russian law. Now sounds dated; superseded by непристойность in modern usage."}
{"term":"бесстыдство","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"besstydstvo","category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"shamelessness","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Shamelessness; brazen sexual or moral indecency performed without any sense of shame; a grave social failure in Russian honor culture.","english_term":"shamelessness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Literally 'without-shame-ness' (без=without, стыд=shame). Its force comes from the honor-shame framework central to traditional Russian culture: стыд is a moral regulator, its absence signals total degradation. The English concept 'indecency' is only approximate — the Russian specifically names the honor-culture violation."}
{"term":"срамные части","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"sramnye chasti","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"genitals_euphemism","concept":"genitalia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":82,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The genitals; literally 'shame parts,' a euphemistic plural noun for the external genitalia of either sex, common in religious and pre-modern texts.","english_term":"private parts","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Срам (sram) = shame in Old Slavonic; shame-based naming of genitalia is widespread across Slavic and biblical traditions. Parallel to Latin pudenda (things to be ashamed of). Appears in Bible translations, folk medicine, and 19th-century literature. Now archaic."}
{"term":"блудный","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"bludny","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"licentiousness","concept":"wanton_behavior","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Licentious or lecherous; the adjective form of блуд, meaning characterized by sexual or moral waywardness and sinful straying.","english_term":"lecherous","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Universally recognizable via 'блудный сын' (the prodigal son), which skews connotation toward 'wayward/straying' rather than purely 'lustful.' The specifically sexual sense is more present in other блуд- compounds. In isolation, typically read as 'promiscuous' or 'licentious.'"}
{"term":"разврат","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"razvrat","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"debauchery","concept":"debauchery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":45,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":95,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Debauchery; moral and sexual corruption and depravity; actively used in modern Russian journalism and political rhetoric to denote sexual licentiousness.","english_term":"debauchery","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Unlike most archaic words in this section, разврат remains in vigorous contemporary use — appearing in news media, political rhetoric (particularly about 'Western разврат'), and casual speech. One of the most universally recognized words in this set; bridges archaic and contemporary registers."}
{"term":"любострастие","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"lyubostrastiye","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sensuality","concept":"sensuality","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":30,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":50,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Voluptuousness or lustfulness; the archaic literary term for deep, sustained surrender to carnal and sensual pleasures.","english_term":"voluptuousness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound of люб- (love/pleasure) + страсть (passion). More literary and elevated than похоть (lust); implies sustained wallowing in pleasure rather than acute desire. Common in 18th–19th century Russian prose, poetry, and translations of French libertine literature."}
{"term":"чресла","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"chresla","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"loins","concept":"genitalia","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":10,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The loins; the area of the hips and lower abdomen biblically associated with reproductive power and physical strength.","english_term":"loins","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Church Slavonic borrowing; direct equivalent of biblical 'loins.' Appears in phrases like 'от чресел своих' (from one's loins = one's offspring) and 'препояши чресла' (gird your loins). Not inherently crude — purely poetic and archaic. The genitalia concept is approximate; чресла denotes the entire loin region, not specifically the genitals."}
{"term":"лоно","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"lono","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"womb_vulva","concept":"vulva","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The womb, bosom, or lap; in literary and archaic use, a euphemism for the female genital region.","english_term":"womb","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary meanings: (1) bosom/lap of nature ('на лоне природы'); (2) womb; (3) in explicit literary use, the vulva or vaginal area. Still widely recognized due to common figurative use. The vulva concept is the most sexually proximate registry match, though лоно primarily means womb or bosom."}
{"term":"плоть","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"plot'","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"carnal_flesh","concept":"carnal_desire","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":10,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Flesh; the physical, carnal body as opposed to the spirit; in religious contexts specifically denotes the sinful sexual and bodily nature of humanity.","english_term":"flesh","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Derived from Church Slavonic; central to Orthodox theology ('грехи плоти' = sins of the flesh; 'умерщвление плоти' = mortification of the flesh). The carnal_desire concept is approximate — плоть is the body itself, not a state of desire — but no registry concept covers 'carnal flesh' as a theological entity."}
{"term":"утеха плоти","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"utekha ploti","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"sensual_pleasure","concept":"sensuality","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":25,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The pleasures of the flesh; carnal or sensual gratification; a literary phrase for sexual and bodily pleasure viewed through a morally censorious lens.","english_term":"pleasures of the flesh","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Утеха (comfort, consolation, pleasure) + плоти (genitive of плоть = flesh). Used in 19th-century literature to describe characters who surrender to bodily pleasures, always with an ironic or condemnatory undertone. The phrase persists in contemporary conservative religious rhetoric."}
{"term":"грехопадение","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"grekhopаdeniye","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sexual_fall","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The fall into sin; in secular literary use, a moral or sexual downfall, particularly a woman's first act of sexual transgression.","english_term":"fall from grace","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primarily refers to the biblical Fall of Adam and Eve (грех=sin, падение=fall). In literary Russian applied to sexual ruin — especially a young woman's first extra-marital sexual act. The sexual sense is heavily gendered: a man's грехопадение is moral; a woman's is specifically sexual ruin and social death."}
{"term":"блудодеяние","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"bludodeyaniye","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"fornication","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":25,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":38,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Fornication; illicit sexual intercourse; a Church Slavonic compound used primarily in religious law and moral theology.","english_term":"fornication","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Combines блуд (fornication/straying) + деяние (act/deed); parallel to любодеяние but with slightly more emphasis on the concrete deed. Appears in Russian Orthodox canon law and pre-revolutionary moral texts. Effectively extinct in modern spoken Russian."}
{"term":"сладострастница","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"sladostrastnitsa","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"voluptuous_woman","concept":"nymphomania","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":35,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":48,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A voluptuous or wantonly sensual woman; a woman who surrenders excessively to sexual and sensual pleasure.","english_term":"voluptuary","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From сладострастие (sweet-passion): сладо- (sweet) + страсть (passion/lust). More literary than pejorative — 19th-century prose uses it for femmes fatales and morally fallen women. The nymphomania concept is approximate; сладострастница implies willing pleasure-seeking, not clinical compulsion. The 'sweet' etymology gives it a more seductive than pathological connotation."}
{"term":"любострастник","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"lyubostrastnik","category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"voluptuous_man","concept":"libertine","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":30,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":45,"recognition_pct_peak":65,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A sensualist or libertine; a man who dedicates himself to the pursuit of sexual and sensual pleasure; the masculine counterpart to сладострастница.","english_term":"libertine","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From любострастие (see above). Used in 18th–19th century literature, often in translations of French libertine novels. The female form сладострастница is more common in Russian literary tradition, reflecting the culture's tendency to focus moral censure on women. The English 'libertine' emphasizes moral defiance; любострастник emphasizes pleasure-seeking."}
{"term":"жить в блуде","lang":"ru","script":"cyrl","romanization":"zhit' v blude","category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"fornication","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":25,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"To live in fornication; to conduct one's life in a persistent state of sexual sin outside of marriage; a biblical phrase for ongoing sexual immorality.","english_term":"to live in sin","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Жить (to live) + в блуде (in fornication/straying). Implies an ongoing sinful state rather than a single act. The English idiom 'living in sin' is approximate — the Russian explicitly names блуд (fornication) rather than sin generically. Used in Orthodox Church contexts and pre-revolutionary folk speech."}
